Yes it is; I recently followed through the control flow for melee damage.
The function dmgval(obj, mon) returns the total damage rolled, based on
the nature of the weapon (obj) and the victim (mon) - that is to say,
the damage dice for the weapon, plus any modifiers for materials or
blessing, plus enchantment.  For artifacts, this value is then modified
by a call to spec_dbon(), which (for a double-damage artifact affecting
all targets) simply doubles the value it is given (side note: a double-
damage artifact will *always* do at least one point of damage, even if
used against shades and not made of silver).

There is *absolutely no way* to get back from the return value of dmgval()
to the damage done by the weapon *without* the silver or blessing or
enchantment bonuses.

Thus, the blessed +7 Grayswandir does 20-78 damage to demons, shades,
and vampires.  It does 16-30 against non-undead non-demonic non-silver-
hating creatures, which still compares favorably with the blessed +7
Mjollnir (10-36 against non-undead non-demonic non-shock-resistant
creatures, dropping to 9-12 against non-undead non-demonic creatures
with shock resistance).

Thus: Grayswandir is the best weapon in the game.

